<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Sook Ching</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1942">1942</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Sook Ching massacre</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sook Ching, meaning &quot;purge through cleansing&quot;, was the mass killing of ethnic Chinese by the Japanese military in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-singapore">fall of Singapore</a> in February 1942. Chinese men were screened at registration centres, and those suspected of anti-Japanese sympathies were taken away and shot. Estimates of the dead range from about 25,000 to 50,000.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sook-ching.jpg" alt="Sook Ching" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Deoma12 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Civilian_War_Memorial_2019.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The Japanese 25th Army saw the Chinese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a> as the likely centre of resistance to Japanese expansion, partly because of the money they had raised for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> after the invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manchuria">Manchuria</a> and during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-sino-japanese-war">Second Sino-Japanese War</a>. Its military government section drew up an &quot;Implementation Guideline for Manipulating Overseas Chinese&quot; on or around 28 December 1941, before the landing, and the chief of staff Sōsaku Suzuki told the Kenpeitai commander Oishi Masayuki to prepare a purge. The historian Hirofumi Hayashi concludes that the massacre was premeditated; the staff officer Tsuji Masanobu is named as its chief planner.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The killings ran from 18 February to 4 March 1942. Oishi, with 200 regular Kenpeitai officers and about 1,000 auxiliaries, ordered Chinese males aged 18 to 50 to report to screening centres. On paper the targets were communists, volunteers who had fought for the British, financiers of Chinese resistance and gangsters, but in practice the selection was arbitrary: hooded informants or officers picked out men at will, and at one centre all who passed through a particular entrance were taken away to be shot. Those released were stamped &quot;examined&quot;; the others were driven by truck to sites such as Changi Beach, Punggol Point and Sentosa.</p><p>At Tsuji's urging the operation was extended to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malaya">Malaya</a>, where the Japanese, lacking time and men to screen a scattered population, carried out indiscriminate massacres, chiefly in the southern states: about 2,000 died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kota-tinggi">Kota Tinggi</a> on 28 February 1942, and 1,474 at Joo Loong Loong on 18 March. Japan later admitted about 6,000 deaths in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lee-kuan-yew">Lee Kuan Yew</a>, who himself nearly became a victim, put the toll far higher.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>In 1947 a British war crimes court tried seven officers; Saburo Kawamura and Masayuki Oishi were hanged on 26 June 1947, while Tsuji escaped, later became a politician in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and disappeared in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laos">Laos</a> in 1961. After mass graves were found at Siglap in 1962, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/singapore">Singapore</a>'s Chinese community boycotted Japanese imports for a week in September 1963. On 25 October 1966 Japan agreed to pay S$50 million, half as a grant and half as a loan, as a gesture of atonement, without an apology or acceptance of legal responsibility. The Civilian War Memorial on Beach Road, unveiled on 15 February 1967, holds the remains found from 1962 to 1966.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sook Ching” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Churchill's 'Gibraltar of the East', the naval base, did not save it: 60,000 British troops surrendered on 15 February 1942, and between 5,000 and 25,000 Chinese were killed in the <b>Sook Ching massacre</b> under Japanese occupation.</blockquote><a href="/empire/singapore">Singapore · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Chinese residents, seen as hostile, were targeted in the <b>Sook Ching massacres</b> of 1942, which killed tens of thousands.</blockquote><a href="/state/japanese-occupation-of-malaya">Japanese occupation of Malaya</a></li></ul></section>
